17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1061 30789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1061 30790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1061 37506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1061 37838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1061 37839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1065 3184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1067 "Addition of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1064 "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A,$ and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix \n![](images/99/991b6f0c69e6341620139db9bfce5303785be21760eb8d630bfee92c57d4bc6a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A,$ we need to find the solution of the system\n\n![](images/1c/1cd56dc26ad4f478c3d7cb14375b8b2e45ef68e3215e79494d56108fecec39cb.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that \n\n![](images/56/56587dc5c1c98493060edb3561c13aa1576187461418619684459dd1f55626fe.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}$.\n\nIn other words,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$  is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1065 3520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7c75fc81-c464-5eea-bb66-37258ca64cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125778 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1064 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1065 3521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e46fe003-9e24-5794-b139-5c2805d463c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125779 #edb/ref 1067 "The Nullity of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/c1/c1f4086a1ffb63c41af0a9b50c37a0c837cff641fbf1f79add41757b86a15919.svg)\n\nwith the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3.$ Note that this eigenvalue has multiplicity two in the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWe know how to find eigenvectors when all eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic equation (i.e., all roots have multiplicity equal to one). But what can we say about the eigenspace $V_{-3}$ for this particular matrix?\n\nRecall that given a matrix with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ is\n\n![](images/dc/dc954c4b48b2ea3bed7a1ab8370d6a2be9a1e22298e9a838acfe47b5b5d0c47d.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of the matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the system, we obtain the general solution \n\n![](images/a5/a552b978409debb7cf783cd683eceda220ba5eb0f37a82b08add9a66106d75fc.svg)\n\nTherefore, our eigenspace is\n\n![](images/87/8767b25332906b9b6149be3a007cbb89e07d791ed58625e9df369880524f2fdb.svg)\n\nNotice that $s=t=0$ gives the zero vector. On the other hand, the vectors from the general solution form a basis $\\mathcal B$ of our eigenspace:\n\n![](images/06/060bba6a9639bb7658256cea78cf6cb362a3a15a435b3391e806ca43742509ed.svg)\n\nIn particular, we have $\\dim(V_{-3}) =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1065 3567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d00ddf6-6fe9-56f2-98f7-dd367ce7b91d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125787 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
